{:edb/db-before-t 930 :edb/db-after-t 931 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534243 50 #inst "2026-10-11T18:09:12.443Z" 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186179731 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415676 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186179731 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415679 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186179731 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415680 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186179731 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415681 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186179731 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415682 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186179733 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415677 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186179733 1038 #edb/ref 17592186415678 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186179861 1050 420.0 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186179996 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177645 13194139534243 17592186045417 false] [17592186179996 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415670 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186179996 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186179996 1071 "![](images/ec/ec03c28e17138917d31898f165925cb246cf1f81a03f15672f87dde586b06ed2.png)\n\nCalculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}dy$, where $C = C_{1}∪C_{2}$ is the path comprising of a horizontal line segment of length $2$ and a semicircle of radius $2$, shown above." 13194139534243 17592186045417 false] [17592186179996 1071 "![](images/ec/ec03c28e17138917d31898f165925cb246cf1f81a03f15672f87dde586b06ed2.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is the path comprising of a horizontal line segment of length $2$ and a semicircle of radius $2,$ shown above." 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186179996 1074 "The line integral of unity with respect to $y$ along a path $C$ is equal to the net change in $y$ between the terminal and initial points:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y={y}_{2}-{y}_{1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${y}_{2}$ is the $y$-coordinate of the terminal point, and ${y}_{1}$ is the $y$-coordinate of the initial point.\n\nIn our case, ${y}_{1}={y}_{2}=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}y={y}_{2}-{y}_{1}=1-1=0.\n$$" 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186180003 1068 #edb/ref 17592186177687 13194139534243 17592186045417 false] [17592186180003 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415668 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186180003 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186180003 1071 "If $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}f(x, y)dy = 12$ and $\\displaystyle \\int_{-C_{1}}f(x, y)dy = 8$, calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_{-C_{2}}f(x, y)dy$ given that $C = C_{1}∪C_{2}$." 13194139534243 17592186045417 false] [17592186180003 1071 "If ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=12$ and ${\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=8,$ calculate ${\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$ given that $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186180003 1074 "Let $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ be a piecewise smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. \n\nFor line integrals with respect to $y,$ we have the following properties:\n\n- ${\\int }_{-C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=-{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y$\n\nIn our case, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\ 12 & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\ 12 & =-8-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\ 20 & =-{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y=-20.\n$$" 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186180715 1064 "Earlier, we showed that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\,\\textrm d x = 2,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the path along the quarter-circle shown below.\n\n![](images/01/01b5f21b2c778f62f7dd884065ee4ce17dd01bd021063fc7c788f09a89feea86.png)\n\nIf we reverse the orientation of the curve, we get the following path:\n\n![](images/46/469675e85a6ca1d23de6da985d36e8e86c859ceff38ea6849984ea3c2cd34abe.png)\n\nThe initial and terminal points have swapped, and so we now have $x_1 = 2$ and $x_2 = 0.$ Applying our formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-C}\\,\\text{d}x & ={x}_{2}-{x}_{1} \\\\  & =0-2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis example illustrates an important property of line integrals with respect to $x,$ described in full as follows:\n\n*Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. Then,*\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{-C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x = -\\int_{C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\n*Similarly, *\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_{-C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y = -\\int_{C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nIn other words, line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y$ are *dependent on orientation*. This is quite different from the case for line integrals with respect to arc length, which are independent of orientation." 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186180715 1064 "Earlier, we showed that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\,\\textrm d x = 2,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the path along the quarter-circle shown below.\n\n![](images/01/01b5f21b2c778f62f7dd884065ee4ce17dd01bd021063fc7c788f09a89feea86.png)\n\nIf we reverse the orientation of the curve, we get the following path:\n\n![](images/46/469675e85a6ca1d23de6da985d36e8e86c859ceff38ea6849984ea3c2cd34abe.png)\n\nThe initial and terminal points have swapped, and so we now have $x_1 = 2$ and $x_2 = 0.$ Applying our formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-C}\\,\\text{d}x & ={x}_{2}-{x}_{1} \\\\  & =0-2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis example illustrates an important property of line integrals with respect to $x,$ described in full as follows:\n\n> *Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth path in the $xy$-plane, and let $-C$ be the curve consisting of the same points as $C$ but with the opposite orientation. Then,*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\displaystyle\\int_{-C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x = -\\int_{C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x.\n> $$\n> \n> \n> *Similarly, *\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\displaystyle\\int_{-C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y = -\\int_{C} f(x,y)\\,\\textrm d y.\n> $$\n\nIn other words, line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y$ are *dependent on orientation*. This is quite different from the case for line integrals with respect to arc length, which are independent of orientation." 13194139534243 17592186045417 false] [17592186180717 1064 "![](images/c2/c2ab6be66d91822186ee94c846f8cd1a247da776b89d7d9220b836193b9b04d3.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ above. Earlier, we showed that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\,\\textrm d x = 2,\n$$\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- In the context of our usual interpretation of line integrals with respect to $x,$ this result represents the total area accumulated when the function $z=1$ is projected onto the plane $y=0$ as $C$ is traversed from the initial point $(0,2)$ to the terminal point $(2,0).$\n\n![](images/29/297e4e302b0cec7f4448ec7e8b36ab6c1f84d79d5f71aad1c6887d86d54dc328.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that this projection generates a rectangle of length $x_2 - x_1 = 2$ and height $z=1.$ Since the integral of unity with respect to $x$ along $C$ is equal to the area of this rectangle, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x & =1\\cdot ({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}) \\\\  & ={x}_{2}-{x}_{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFurthermore, this result is *positive* because the rectangle is traced out in the direction of *increasing* $x.$\n\n- If we reverse the direction of the curve then we get a *negative* result. This is because the rectangle is traced out in the direction of *decreasing* $x,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/17/179713734445f85818ef4e46cfa5948115cea936538acd3095cbfa706d381972.png)" 13194139534243 17592186045417 false] [17592186180717 1064 "![](images/c2/c2ab6be66d91822186ee94c846f8cd1a247da776b89d7d9220b836193b9b04d3.png)\n\nConsider the path $C$ above. Earlier, we showed that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C}\\,\\textrm d x = 2,\n$$\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- In the context of our usual interpretation of line integrals with respect to $x,$ this result represents the total area accumulated when the function $z=1$ is projected onto the plane $y=0$ as $C$ is traversed from the initial point $(0,2)$ to the terminal point $(2,0).$ \n\n![](images/29/297e4e302b0cec7f4448ec7e8b36ab6c1f84d79d5f71aad1c6887d86d54dc328.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that this projection generates a rectangle of length $x_2 - x_1 = 2$ and height $z=1.$ Since the integral of unity with respect to $x$ along $C$ is equal to the area of this rectangle, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}x & =1\\cdot ({x}_{2}-{x}_{1}) \\\\  & ={x}_{2}-{x}_{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFurthermore, this result is *positive* because the rectangle is traced out in the direction of *increasing* $x.$\n\n- If we reverse the direction of the curve then we get a *negative* result. This is because the rectangle is traced out in the direction of *decreasing* $x,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/17/179713734445f85818ef4e46cfa5948115cea936538acd3095cbfa706d381972.png)" 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186415622 1080 "-19" 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186415622 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186415622 1083 #uuid "01e4d0b1-3d49-5ae5-b65b-8dbe801177f5" 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186415623 1080 "5" 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186415623 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186415623 1083 #uuid "05561efb-810b-5987-b7e5-1cc8fd5d9c6a" 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186415624 1080 "-1" 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186415624 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534243 17592186045417 true] [17592186415624 1083 #uuid "105bce54-4026-504a-aebc